00:00:02.230 --> 00:00:04.550 align:start position:0%
more than 11 million people around the  globe are visually impaired or blind as  a result of a condition called  age-related macular degeneration or amd  as the world population ages this number  is projected to rise to over 18 million  people by the year 2040  advanced amd comes in two forms wet and  dry and it's not clear why people  develop one type instead of another  without treatment both types can  ultimately lead to blindness  so what happens in the eye to cause this  loss of vision  in a healthy retina rods and cones your  photoreceptors translate light from the  outside world into electrical signals to  send to the brain  these rods and cones sit on the retinal  pigment epithelium or rpe  beneath that is brook's membrane which  regulates the flow of nutrients and  waste products between the epithelium  and the underlying blood vessels  this is one of the places amd most  likely begins in the collection of small  blood vessels called the coriocalaris  as our eyes age these blood vessels  become more sparse this begins to happen  much more quickly in people with dry amd  next deposits of lipids proteins and  minerals called drusen start to form  within brook's membrane and as the  disease progresses these deposits grow  and block nutrients which were headed  for the rpe  the drusen also prevent waste products  from being moved away  without good nutrient supply and with  toxins building up the rpe degrades  to make matters worse drusen contain  proteins that can activate immune cells  causing an inflammatory response  heightened inflammation contributes to  more blood vessel loss and that in turn  starves the rpe further  and as a result starves the  photoreceptors of vital nutrients  as dry amd progresses cells begin to  detach from brook's membrane  this leads to blindness  there is no cure for dry amd at the  moment but a good number of treatments  are currently being trialled  dead photoreceptors can't be replaced  but a type of cell therapy aims to  repopulate lost rpe cells and keep  whatever photoreceptors do remain  healthier for longer  to do that healthy rpe cells grown from  stem cells are either injected into the  retina or embedded in a brook like  membrane which can then be implanted  these new rpe cells help to preserve any  remaining photoreceptors and can hold  back the tide of destruction that amd  causes  other treatments aim to prevent the  worst of the damage before it even  occurs  some of these aim to reduce build-up of  toxic waste products or to make the  photoreceptors more resilient better  able to hold on tightly in the face of  degradation of rpe cells  or they aim to interfere with the  protein pathway that revs up the  inflammatory response  none of these therapeutic approaches  have been through all the required  stages of clinical trials to become  widely available to patients but any one  of them or perhaps a combination of  several could prove to be life-changing  for people around the globe with dry amd
 

